1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,700
Imagine a birthday party.

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:03,180
The girl is six.

3
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:04,120
There are forty people

4
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:04,880
in the room,

5
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:05,640
and thirty of them

6
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:06,640
are her parents,

7
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:08,180
her grandparents, and the grandparents

8
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:09,860
of her grandparents.

9
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:10,900
The oldest guest is a hundred

10
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:12,680
and twenty-six.

11
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:13,460
He is not the one

12
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:14,500
in the wheelchair.

13
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:16,460
He's the one on the bouncy castle.

14
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:17,280
This is what the world looks

15
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:18,700
like when humans live to one

16
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,700
hundred and fifty.

17
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:22,180
And no, it isn't a world

18
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:23,900
of very old people.

19
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,240
It's a world where nobody leaves.

20
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:27,060
By the end,

21
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:28,020
you'll see why,

22
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:28,700
in this world,

23
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:30,400
the healthiest mother on the street

24
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:33,020
is her children's biggest problem.

25
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:34,760
It starts quietly.

26
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:36,980
One morning, ageing slows down,

27
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:38,900
for everyone, from wherever their body

28
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:40,460
already is.

29
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:42,600
A man blows out forty candles,

30
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:44,420
same as last year.

31
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:45,280
He'll now make it to about

32
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:47,000
a hundred and thirty.

33
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:48,600
His father, who's seventy,

34
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:50,560
gets twenty-odd bonus years.

35
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:51,380
And the baby born

36
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:54,060
in the hospital across town that morning?

37
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:55,220
She's the first human who will

38
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:57,600
ever see one hundred and fifty.

39
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:00,040
Across town, at a pension company,

40
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:01,240
someone changes one number

41
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:02,980
on a spreadsheet.

42
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,200
Eighty becomes one hundred and fifty.

43
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:07,360
Every cell below it turns red.

44
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:11,500
Because here's the thing we never notice.

45
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:13,480
The whole world is built around

46
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:15,600
people leaving.

47
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:17,700
You work for about forty years,

48
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:19,040
then you stop.

49
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:20,540
Your parents pass away,

50
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:21,520
and in your fifties,

51
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:22,880
you get the house.

52
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:24,200
The boss retires,

53
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:26,140
and somebody gets promoted.

54
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:27,760
Every few decades,

55
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:29,380
the jobs, the homes,

56
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:30,920
the money and the power all

57
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:32,700
come free again.

58
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:34,060
Death is the thing that moves

59
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:35,120
the queue.

60
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:36,120
Take that away,

61
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:38,160
and the queue stops moving.

62
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:39,440
And the first place you notice

63
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:41,580
is the family photo.

64
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:43,020
Today, a big family photo

65
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:44,580
has three rows.

66
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:45,980
Grandparents at the back,

67
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:47,060
parents in the middle,

68
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:48,540
kids on the floor.

69
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:49,400
In this world,

70
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:51,680
you retake it every twenty years,

71
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:52,920
and every time,

72
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:54,560
the frame gets wider.

73
00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:56,140
By the time that baby

74
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:57,660
from the hospital is a great-great-grandmother,

75
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:00,860
it's five rows deep.

76
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:03,360
Five generations, all alive,

77
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:05,880
all healthy, all arguing about where

78
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:06,840
to sit.

79
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:08,560
And look at the front row.

80
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:10,540
It's almost empty.

81
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:12,020
If everyone lives twice as long

82
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:13,400
and keeps having kids at today's

83
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:15,440
pace, the planet runs out of room.

84
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:16,820
So families shrink.

85
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:18,340
The world grows to about nineteen

86
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:20,420
billion people, roughly double today,

87
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,020
and then it stops.

88
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:23,020
Children go from one in every

89
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:25,120
four people to one in eight.

90
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:27,200
A kid in this world is rare.

91
00:02:27,220 --> 00:02:28,700
She is outnumbered by her own

92
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:31,680
ancestors at her own birthday party.

93
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:33,080
And if the family photo gets

94
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:34,820
longer, so does the marriage

95
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:36,800
in the middle of it.

96
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:38,440
Today, "till death do us part"

97
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:40,560
means about fifty years.

98
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:41,620
Here, marry at thirty,

99
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:43,100
and death is a hundred

100
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:45,120
and twenty years away.

101
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:46,460
That's a very long time

102
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:48,360
to agree on a thermostat.

103
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:49,860
So the vows change.

104
00:02:49,880 --> 00:02:51,200
Couples start writing an end date

105
00:02:51,220 --> 00:02:52,140
into them.

106
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:53,360
At the jeweller's,

107
00:02:53,380 --> 00:02:54,460
wedding rings come engraved

108
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:55,340
with a year,

109
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:56,280
the way milk comes

110
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:57,700
with a date.

111
00:02:57,720 --> 00:02:58,820
And Grandma's third wedding

112
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:00,600
is a lovely day.

113
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:02,940
Her first husband gives a speech.

114
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:04,720
Longer marriages are a choice.

115
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:06,720
Longer careers aren't.

116
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:08,800
Today, you start work at twenty,

117
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:10,060
stop at sixty-five,

118
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:12,520
and have about fifteen years left.

119
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:14,480
Here, stopping at sixty-five would leave

120
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:16,820
you eighty-five years with no pay.

121
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:18,640
Nobody can save for that.

122
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:19,440
So the retirement age

123
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:20,180
has to move,

124
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:21,640
and once you do the sums,

125
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:22,780
it lands at about one hundred

126
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:24,880
and twenty.

127
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:27,280
Picture your first payslip at twenty.

128
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:28,100
Near the bottom,

129
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:29,480
a new line:

130
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:32,280
years to retirement, ninety-four.

131
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:33,440
But a hundred-year career

132
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:34,980
has a problem.

133
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,180
Whatever you learned at twenty

134
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:38,180
is useless by sixty.

135
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:39,700
So people go back to school,

136
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:41,560
again and again.

137
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:42,740
In a lecture hall,

138
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:44,200
a seventy-year-old sits next

139
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:45,700
to an eighteen-year-old.

140
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:46,820
Same course.

141
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:48,040
Same exam.

142
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:50,020
The seventy-year-old has fifty more years

143
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:51,660
of work ahead.

144
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:53,920
The eighteen-year-old has a hundred.

145
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:57,860
The seventy-year-old's CV is eleven pages long.

146
00:03:57,880 --> 00:03:59,020
And a hundred years of work

147
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:00,500
means a hundred years of paying

148
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:01,880
for a home.

149
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,540
You'd think time would make everyone rich.

150
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:05,840
Give your savings a century

151
00:04:05,860 --> 00:04:06,880
and a half to grow,

152
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:09,460
and a small pot becomes a fortune.

153
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:11,160
But everyone has the same idea,

154
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:12,580
all at once.

155
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:13,860
Open a savings book in this

156
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:15,280
world and watch the rate

157
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:16,020
on each new page.

158
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:18,340
It keeps getting smaller.

159
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:19,400
So people borrow instead,

160
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:22,280
and the banks invent the hundred-year mortgage.

161
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:23,360
It sounds cheap.

162
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:24,800
It isn't.

163
00:04:24,820 --> 00:04:26,760
On a three hundred thousand dollar

164
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:27,480
house, the monthly payment drops

165
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:29,660
by less than three hundred dollars.

166
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:30,700
But you pay three

167
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:32,880
and a half times as much interest.

168
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:34,940
You're not really paying off the house.

169
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:36,420
You're renting it from the bank,

170
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:37,600
for the rest of your very

171
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:38,780
long life.

172
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:40,640
A letter arrives.

173
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:41,900
Payment eight hundred and forty-seven

174
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:44,040
of one thousand, two hundred.

175
00:04:44,060 --> 00:04:45,660
Which is why everyone is waiting

176
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:46,980
for the other way to get

177
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:47,960
a house.

178
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:49,760
The one you inherit.

179
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:51,340
Today, you get your parents' home

180
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:52,920
in your fifties.

181
00:04:52,940 --> 00:04:54,260
Here, your parents live to one

182
00:04:54,280 --> 00:04:56,540
hundred and fifty.

183
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:58,480
They're thirty years older than you.

184
00:04:58,500 --> 00:04:59,240
So you get the house

185
00:04:59,260 --> 00:05:01,880
at one hundred and twenty.

186
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:04,220
Picture the queue for one house:

187
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:05,940
every heir waiting for someone

188
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:08,380
to die, stretching down the street

189
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:10,100
and round the block.

190
00:05:10,120 --> 00:05:12,040
Walk down Marrow Street.

191
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:13,120
Forty houses.

192
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:14,540
Each front door is painted

193
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:16,420
with the year it was bought.

194
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:17,520
Not one of them has changed

195
00:05:17,540 --> 00:05:19,700
hands in ninety years.

196
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:21,720
Today, about one home in forty-five

197
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:24,720
comes free each year when somebody dies.

198
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:25,960
Here, it's one in a hundred

199
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:27,620
and fifteen.

200
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:29,040
And there are twice as many

201
00:05:29,060 --> 00:05:30,520
people who need one.

202
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:32,640
A brass key hangs inside one

203
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:33,880
of those doors.

204
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:35,160
It has a paper tag,

205
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:37,500
for writing each owner's name.

206
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:38,580
It's had one name

207
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:40,760
on it for a very long time.

208
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:42,440
So the young do something clever,

209
00:05:42,460 --> 00:05:44,620
and a little bit dark.

210
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:46,520
At twenty-five, they sell their future

211
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:47,800
inheritance to a bank,

212
00:05:47,820 --> 00:05:49,880
for cash today.

213
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:51,300
The bank now owns the right

214
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:52,940
to Mum's house.

215
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:53,740
And every year,

216
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:55,260
on Mum's birthday,

217
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:57,120
the bank sends her a card.

218
00:05:57,140 --> 00:05:59,160
It asks how she's feeling.

219
00:05:59,180 --> 00:06:00,280
The same queue that blocks

220
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:02,740
the front door blocks the top floor.

221
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:03,720
On the fortieth floor

222
00:06:03,740 --> 00:06:05,280
of the Greyfield Exchange,

223
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:08,200
the desk nameplates are engraved in brass.

224
00:06:08,220 --> 00:06:09,740
Nobody prints them anymore.

225
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:12,920
Nobody has moved desks in eighty years.

226
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:14,060
Today, a top boss keeps

227
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:16,200
the job for about seven years.

228
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:17,720
Here, the same boss can stay

229
00:06:17,740 --> 00:06:18,840
for forty.

230
00:06:18,860 --> 00:06:20,160
Take five hundred of the biggest

231
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:21,580
jobs in a country.

232
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:23,260
Today, about seventy of them open

233
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:24,400
up every year.

234
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:26,340
Here, it's about twelve.

235
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:28,180
Outside one corner office,

236
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:29,520
a man in a sharp suit

237
00:06:29,540 --> 00:06:30,900
sits on a bench.

238
00:06:30,920 --> 00:06:32,500
He's ninety-one.

239
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:33,820
He's been waiting for this job

240
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:35,580
since he was fifty.

241
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:36,540
Every ten years,

242
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:38,420
he asks the same thing.

243
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:40,880
"Is he in?" He's in.

244
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:42,400
The same goes for judges,

245
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:44,640
for professors, for anyone who holds

246
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:47,320
a seat until they choose to leave.

247
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:48,740
In a courthouse hallway,

248
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:51,100
six portraits hang side by side.

249
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:52,680
It's the same judge.

250
00:06:52,700 --> 00:06:54,820
Each one is a little greyer.

251
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:56,920
So ambition leaves.

252
00:06:56,940 --> 00:06:58,160
If you can't climb the old

253
00:06:58,180 --> 00:07:00,660
ladder, you build a new one.

254
00:07:00,680 --> 00:07:02,700
Every founder is under forty,

255
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:04,380
because only a brand new company

256
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:06,060
has no queue.

257
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:07,500
And the one thing young people

258
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:09,880
vote for, over and over,

259
00:07:09,900 --> 00:07:10,600
is term limits.

260
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:12,440
On everything.

261
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:14,200
Now, to be fair,

262
00:07:14,220 --> 00:07:16,400
some of this is wonderful.

263
00:07:16,420 --> 00:07:19,720
A surgeon with ninety years of practice.

264
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:21,280
A teacher who taught your grandmother,

265
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:23,440
and now teaches you,

266
00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:25,320
and still remembers what your grandmother

267
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:27,620
was like at your age.

268
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:29,060
Longer lives have always made

269
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:30,640
the world richer.

270
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:32,180
That part is true.

271
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:35,080
But only if the old let go.

272
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:37,900
And that's where it turns upside down.

273
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:40,060
Remember the bank's birthday card?

274
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:42,280
Every year it asks Mum how she's feeling.

275
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:43,340
And every year,

276
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:46,980
she writes back: never better.

277
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,280
Every one of those cards

278
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:51,420
is another year her children rent.

279
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:52,600
In this world,

280
00:07:52,620 --> 00:07:53,780
your parents' long,

281
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:56,080
happy, healthy life is your single

282
00:07:56,100 --> 00:07:57,720
biggest bill.

283
00:07:57,740 --> 00:07:59,560
It's the one thing nobody says

284
00:07:59,580 --> 00:08:02,020
out loud at the birthday party.

285
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:04,500
And then there's death itself.

286
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:05,620
It doesn't vanish.

287
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:07,480
It changes shape.

288
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:08,700
You still get old.

289
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:09,540
It just starts around one hundred

290
00:08:09,560 --> 00:08:11,380
and thirteen.

291
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:13,420
Your knees hand in their notice.

292
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:14,800
You slow down for your last

293
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:17,700
thirty-odd years, instead of your last fifteen.

294
00:08:17,720 --> 00:08:20,300
But the biggest killer isn't age anymore.

295
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:21,860
It's the road.

296
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:23,560
A car crash at forty used

297
00:08:23,580 --> 00:08:25,420
to cost forty years.

298
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:27,760
Now it costs one hundred and ten.

299
00:08:27,780 --> 00:08:29,820
People start driving like they

300
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:30,720
have a lot to lose.

301
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:33,300
Because they do.

302
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:35,500
St Aldric's crematorium only needs half

303
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:36,980
its building now.

304
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,300
The other half becomes a nursery.

305
00:08:39,320 --> 00:08:40,260
One chimney.

306
00:08:40,280 --> 00:08:41,340
One playground.

307
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:42,820
One car park.

308
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:44,800
And the undertaker's apprentice has been

309
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:46,620
training for sixty years.

310
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:48,980
He's never actually seen a body.

311
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,300
Remember the girl at the birthday party?

312
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:53,420
She's one hundred and twenty now.

313
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:54,800
She has a job,

314
00:08:54,820 --> 00:08:57,840
a mortgage, and a son who's ninety.

315
00:08:57,860 --> 00:08:58,920
This morning, her mother died,

316
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:00,940
peacefully, in the garden,

317
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:04,180
at one hundred and fifty.

318
00:09:04,200 --> 00:09:05,500
She walks up to a door

319
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:07,320
on Marrow Street.

320
00:09:07,340 --> 00:09:10,700
The brass key is still hanging inside.

321
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:11,620
Under her mother's name,

322
00:09:11,640 --> 00:09:12,940
on a tag that has waited

323
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:15,820
a hundred and fifteen years,

324
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:17,420
she writes a second one.

325
00:09:17,440 --> 00:09:18,660
Her own.

326
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:19,760
We like to think a life

327
00:09:19,780 --> 00:09:20,800
is a ladder.

328
00:09:20,820 --> 00:09:22,260
You start at the bottom,

329
00:09:22,280 --> 00:09:24,700
you climb, you reach the top.

330
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:26,280
It was always a queue.

331
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:27,600
And the only thing that ever

332
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:29,340
moved it was death.
